```

`collectIteratedInstances` exists so the container is walked ONCE per call
rather than once per requested id — the original code re-walked it inside the
id loop, which is O(n·m) on a large scene.


THE FIVE CALL SITES
-------------------
Each replaced its own ad-hoc loop with:

```diff
-  const instancesToModify = [];
-  for (const instanceId of existingInstanceIds) { ...startsWith... }
+  const iteratedInstances = collectIteratedInstances(initialInstances);
+  const resolution = resolveExistingInstanceIds(
+    iteratedInstances,
+    existingInstanceIds
+  );
+  if (!resolution.ok) return makeFailure(resolution.error);
```

1. `InstanceTools.js` — 2D modify (`put_2d_instances`)
2. `InstanceTools.js` — 2D erase (`delete_instances`)
3. `EditorFunctions/index.js` — `put_3d_instances` erase
4. `EditorFunctions/index.js` — `put_3d_instances` modify
5. `EditorFunctions/index.js` — instance-variables scope

Note 3–5 are NOT in the file the original audit pointed at: the same pattern
was found alive in `EditorFunctions/index.js` and fixed there too. Reviewers
should check all five, not two.


TESTS
-----
Added to `InstanceTools.spec.js`, and asserted at the shared resolver:
- an ambiguous prefix mutates NOTHING — asserted for 2D, for 3D, and for the
  instance-variables path, because "the right fix in three places" is exactly
  the claim that regresses;
- a full uuid still resolves (regression);
- a uniquely-matching prefix still resolves (regression — this is the case the
  truncation exists for, and it must keep working or the feature regresses);
- an unknown id still produces the not-found failure (regression).
